]\n  Source: Granulomatous liver diseases: a review. > Evaluation\n  Primary biliary cholangitis, formerly known as primary biliary cirrhosis, is another common cause of granulomatous hepatitis in the US. It is an autoimmune chronic liver disease commonly seen in middl\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.79):]\n  Source: Progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis: diagnosis, management, and treatment. > History and Physical\n  Patients with primary biliary cholangitis have various clinical findings on physical exam, which usually correlate with the stage of disease at presentation. About 40% of patients have skin complaints\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  poorly controlled GERD and abdominal pain → hypercalcemia-induced gastrointestinal dysfunction → Multiple Myeloma with Hypercalcemia and Elevated ALP\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate serum calcium levels and parathyroid hormone to determine if hypercalcemia is contributing to uncontrolled GERD and abdominal pain, which would support neoplastic causes like multiple myeloma\n  non-smoker with chronic cough and weight loss → paraneoplastic syndrome in hematologic malignancy → Lymphoma with Paraneoplastic Hypercalcemia and Hepatic ALP Elevation\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Investigate for lymphoma through imaging (e.g. CT) and bone marrow biopsy, particularly given the combination of non-smoking-related cough and unexplained weight loss\n  abdominal pain and 5-pound weight loss → bony metastases with secondary hypercalcemia → Carcinoma with Bony Metastases and Skeletal Hypercalcemia\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Perform bone imaging (e.g. PET/CT) to detect potential bony metastases that could explain both the abdominal pain and systemic symptoms\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
